Title: "Series and Singularities"
Id: "1b15071a-15ba-4c8d-9548-2c33c5faece3"


Text: "Power series provide the local language of holomorphic functions. Laurent
       series enlarge that language by allowing negative powers, which record
       the exact obstruction to holomorphic extension across an isolated point.
       The coefficient of $(z-z_0)^{-1}$ is the residue and controls the
       integral around the singularity."
Id: "f047339a-9031-4ad2-a385-53e1b9f6fe04"


SectionTitle: "Power Series and Analyticity"
Id: "bbd4a392-3dce-42b4-a876-726a3f1654ca"


TextDefinition: "A complex power series centered at $z_0$ is an expression
                 $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^n$. There is a radius
                 $R\in[0,\infty]$ such that the series converges absolutely for
                 $|z-z_0|<R$ and diverges for $|z-z_0|>R$. On every closed disk
                 of radius less than $R$, convergence is uniform, so the sum may
                 be differentiated and integrated term by term."
Id: "64a14bde-975e-4814-99e7-6a749eaeee41"


TextDefinition: "A function is analytic at $z_0$ if it agrees with a convergent
                 power series on some neighborhood of $z_0$. Analyticity is a
                 local property: both the coefficients and the radius may depend
                 on the center. The coefficients are uniquely determined by
                 $a_n=f^{(n)}(z_0)/n!$."
Id: "15856db1-4ad8-4d94-821c-d5f3d9027940"


TextTheorem: "Every holomorphic function is analytic. If $f$ is holomorphic on
              an open set containing the closed disk $\overline{D(z_0,r)}$, then
              for $|z-z_0|<r$,
              $f(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}f^{(n)}(z_0)(z-z_0)^n/n!$. This theorem
              has no direct real-variable analogue: real differentiability of
              every order does not imply real analyticity."
Id: "10dadf20-bc3b-4985-8b87-dd52fd8ba827"


TextTheorem: "Identity theorem. Let $U$ be a connected open subset of
              $\mathbb{C}$ and let $f,g:U\to\mathbb{C}$ be holomorphic. If the
              set $\{z\in U:f(z)=g(z)\}$ has an accumulation point in $U$, then
              $f=g$ throughout $U$. In particular, a nonzero holomorphic
              function has isolated zeros."
Id: "392fe78d-e555-4b9b-a30c-ec368f587045"


SectionTitle: "Laurent Series and Isolated Singularities"
Id: "771a6189-5597-4bbf-8e3a-be234acd8f58"


TextDefinition: "A Laurent series about $z_0$ is
                 $\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}a_n(z-z_0)^n$. If $f$ is holomorphic
                 on an annulus $r<|z-z_0|<R$, it has a unique Laurent expansion
                 converging on that annulus. The negative powers constitute the
                 principal part."
Id: "d1d92ff7-130c-4c29-8b07-11fdc199c29f"


TextDefinition: "Suppose $f$ is holomorphic on a punctured neighborhood of
                 $z_0$. The point is a removable singularity if $f$ extends
                 holomorphically across $z_0$, a pole of order $m$ if its
                 Laurent principal part has terms through $(z-z_0)^{-m}$ and no
                 lower power, and an essential singularity if infinitely many
                 negative-power coefficients are nonzero. These three cases are
                 mutually exclusive and exhaustive."
Id: "2dbcb30d-9d35-4727-ada0-f26dc5938c5e"


TextTheorem: "Riemann's removable singularity theorem. If $f$ is holomorphic on
              a punctured neighborhood of $z_0$ and bounded near $z_0$, then $f$
              has a unique holomorphic extension to $z_0$. Equivalently, the
              principal part of its Laurent expansion vanishes."
Id: "16d31e40-1f3b-4748-8049-9764bf5da80b"


TextTheorem: "Casorati--Weierstrass theorem. Near an essential singularity, the
              image of every punctured neighborhood is dense in $\mathbb{C}$.
              Picard's great theorem strengthens this: with at most one
              exceptional value, every complex value is assumed infinitely often
              in every punctured neighborhood."
Id: "3e6f72a7-a824-480c-896c-9f2e498639c0"


SectionTitle: "Residues"
Id: "2525c045-e366-48e5-ab70-95fde1c155a5"


TextDefinition: "The residue of $f$ at an isolated singularity $z_0$ is the
                 coefficient $a_{-1}$ in its Laurent expansion, written
                 $\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)$. Equivalently, for a sufficiently
                 small positively oriented circle $\gamma$ around $z_0$,
                 $\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}
                 \int_\gamma f(z)\,dz$."
Id: "35848afa-3031-4efe-9d76-d1b297bae371"


TextTheorem: "If $f$ has a pole of order $m$ at $z_0$, then
              $\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=\frac{1}{(m-1)!}
              \lim_{z\to z_0}\frac{d^{m-1}}{dz^{m-1}}
              ((z-z_0)^m f(z))$. For a simple pole represented as $f=g/h$ with
              $h(z_0)=0$ and $h'(z_0)\ne0$, this reduces to
              $\operatorname{Res}(f,z_0)=g(z_0)/h'(z_0)$."
Id: "324cfa4b-56db-4bab-804d-721de64b087e"


Text: "Residues compress local singular behavior into the single coefficient
       detected by contour integration. The residue theorem is therefore a
       local-to-global statement: a boundary integral is the sum of finitely
       many local contributions from the singularities enclosed by the contour."
Id: "d9f17a2f-2d5a-44e6-857e-9ca53b40f744"
